  "notes": "Street to the north, a reservoir to the south. From the overhead drone view (two stone bedroom wings under shallow dark metal gables either side of a sunken courtyard, a central flat roof with the round skylight over the helical stair, the long living wing under the big dark gable, the pool beside it and the pale flat roof over the gallery), the oblique drone view, the lakeside elevations (glazed living floor with board-formed concrete ends over a stone lower floor with its own glass wall, raised pool on a stone plinth, steps down to a sunken lounge) and the entrance views (a curving steel-grating bridge with timber slat parapets crossing from street level to the door). The living wing is in fact turned a few degrees off the others; boxes can't rotate, so it is squared up. The bridge's curve is stepped. Slope simplified to a raised street strip; lower floors of the wings are shown full height. Dark-grey standing-seam roofs drawn as dark; pale gallery roof as metal. Pass of 24 Sep 2026: the walls are the dark grey-brown field stone of the photographs, now rubble (was the paler stone); the bare winter trees (the drone views, the lakeside elevations) are trunks with two limbs and open crowns of mesh blobs instead of solid timber balls, one evergreen and the small shrubs kept; the ground is the dry winter grass of every exterior view (drygrass over the plate), with green lawn only where the lakeside photos show it (below the sunken lounge, and east of the living wing); feather-grass beds along the entry path; the pool is a raised pool let into the stone plinth at the upper floor with a white concrete rim; the bridge is a steel-grating deck (mesh) with timber slat parapets laid in 1 m steps along an S — swinging west past the street end and easing back between the wings, as in the overhead view. Still stepped at 0.5 m voxels; the bridge is now a SketchUp mesh (see below). Still guessed: the slope (a raised street strip), the living wing's few-degree turn (squared up), the lower floors' full extent. Refined 24 Sep 2026 against the overhead drone view: the roofs read as the mid-grey ribbed standing seam of the photographs, drawn as an open mesh sheet one voxel over the dark gable so the dots mix to grey and fall in seam-like streaks (plain dark read near-black; solid rib boxes were tried and read as fat tubes, so were dropped). Refined 22 Oct 2026 (3 rounds, photos 00-23; the five biggest mismatches were the bridge, the roofs' light colour, the white holes in the roofs, the planting at the bridge and the living wing's turn, which boxes cannot draw): the bridge, a stepped zigzag of 1 m boxes, is now a smooth S built in SketchUp (scripts/sketchup/unpuente_bridge.py, sketchup/unpuente-bridge.skp, read back as two meshes: the steel-grating deck 1.7 m wide and the timber parapets drawn 0.5 m thick, a voxel, so they do not fall between the columns). It follows the overhead photograph (02): straight along the east side of the lawn from the street, swinging west between the stone wings and bowing back to the door, 1.2 m clear between parapets, at street level, about 25 m long; the roofs' second layer is solid slate over the dark one, where the open mesh let the white of the page through in streaks and read light grey: they read dark charcoal as in photographs 01 and 02; the feather-grass bed the bridge crosses (gravel with seven tussocks and a boulder). Still guessed: the S's exact swing (read off a straight-down photograph at about 33 px a metre), the living wing's few degrees of turn (squared up), the slope (a raised street strip), the lower floors' extent, the standing seams (not drawn)."
